A||N||9||FieroSTETZ||stupid exhaust manifolds - possible to remove w/out removing cradle?||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||07-15-2001||03:44 AM||||is it possible to remove the firewall side exhaust manifold without dropping the cradle? haven't had the time yet to try, anyone know?||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||07-15-2001||04:19 AM||||Possible? Yes....easy to do? <FONT COLOR="red"><FONT SIZE="3">NO!!!</FONT></FONT><BR>Ive done it before on my 87 GT...without a doubt the most pain in the a$$ thing Ive ever had to do on a Fiero...<BR>There will undoubtably be somebody on here that says "oh its not that hard"...uh huh...<BR>Anyway it is possible..but a major pain...good luck. <FONT COLOR="blue"><FONT SIZE="3"><B>7</FONT></FONT></B>||||reg||11
Z||000002||grinthock||07-15-2001||08:34 AM||||BanditBalz does it once a year and says it's pretty easy maybe he has some sort of special method.||||reg||1
Z||000003||wkayl||07-15-2001||10:44 AM||||I just pulled the front manifold off my daughter's 88 Formula yesterday.  Worst part is A/C compressor.  Liquid wrench works wonders on rusted exhaust bolts.   It's not fun, but can be done.   <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_2.jpg"> <BR>Dnyee's 88 Formula, Casey's 86GT, Wayne's 86GT.  <BR>We consider my wife as being "Fiero Challenged"||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||07-15-2001||10:52 AM||||It can be done but like folks say it is a PAIN. It is alot easier if the car has either no A/C or you drop the compressor. When I took mine off I had to get some bolts from the top and some from the bottom. Going from the bottom, you need to reach around the A/C compressor, the Cat, and the coolant tube. Lot's of Liquid wrench is required. If you break a bolt, it is very hard to get it out.<BR>Once you remove it and you see where you had to go to access bolts, putting on the new ones (or replacing the old) is easier. I would also get new bolts.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 07-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||PBJ||07-15-2001||11:12 AM||||once that cat has been removed its all pretty straight forward.  I would say it is easyer that most fwd cars.||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||07-15-2001||12:00 PM||||I just did mine (1987 GT).  Got them all from the top.  Suggest you remove the A/C compressor, which (for me, at least) means you jack up the car, remove the RR wheel, plastic inner fender and so forth.<P>Now, since the car's up in the air anyway, you might as well remove the aft (2) cradle bolts, and swing the aft end of the cradle down, to give yourself more room.  Yes, I've read the horror stories about breaking loose the welded-on nuts in the frame.  I guess I'm just lucky - I've never had the pleasure  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Those nuts (so far) have always cooperated with me.<P>Others have suggested lowering the forward end of the cradle.  I bet that works great, too, but I've never tried it.  I know the GM recall instructions suggest lowering the front end of the cradle.<P>What everybody said about the Liquid Wrench.  Procrastination was my ally - every time I thought, "I really should get after that manifold," I'd think again about how much fun it was going to be, and squirt more Liquid Wrench on all the bolts.  And then go watch TV, mow the lawn- whatever.<P>BTW, doing the manifold bolts from the top, I stood in the trunk, leaning over the engine.  Knee pads are great, especially for an old relic like myself  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Also good if there are some well-behaved, "Wanna help" type kids in the neighborhood who wander into the garage, and hand you tools (saves climbing in and out of trunk)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<P>I wouldn't go as far as to say it's easy.  But it's not the worst task on earth, either.  Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||ThatRickGuy||07-15-2001||12:48 PM||||It matters on how the car is set up. My car, it would probrably take 15 minutes on a lift to get it out. No A/C, cat eliminator pipe. but with the cat and A/C, I'd say lift the car, drop the exhaust, unbolt the A/C, and you should have a clear run at the whole thing. One suggestion though, pull the battery before you jump in, there are 2 bolts on the manifold that'll put your ratchet right on the positive terminal from the starter if you slip.||||reg||1
Z||000008||bilbo||07-17-2001||01:25 PM||||Definately the way to go-remove exhaust and compressor.Saves time and much easier,I just finished pulling my manifolds and definately recommend doing it this way!||||reg||1
Z||000009||rc5star||07-17-2001||03:42 PM||||I bought a set of "S" shaped wrenches from Autozone for $16 and after soaking the bolts in PB Blaster they worked great in getting in those tight places by the compressor.||||reg||1
